cally make boxing unethical. What matters is whether the intentional act is part of a consensual, regulated, and valued human activity—and whether the participants understand and accept the risks. Boxing meets that standard. That said, I respect that for some people, the line will always be drawn differently. And that's a reasonable ethical position to hold. But I believe a sport can be ethical even when it involves intentional, risky contact—as long as the intent is not to cause lasting harm, and the participants freely choose to engage.
