el that intentionally punching someone in the head is fundamentally wrong," I can't fully argue them out of that intuition. It's a reasonable ethical line to draw. What I can say is that many people—including doctors, ethicists, and former boxers—believe the sport can be practiced ethically, with proper safeguards, and that the benefits for participants are real. So I don't think you're wrong to feel uneasy. I just think the ethical picture is complex enough that a thoughtful person can land on either side. My position is that boxing *can* be ethical—not that it always is, or that the concerns are trivial.
